      "why": "The spec requires 32 results/us throughput and a 0.44us p99 batch latency for 32 simultaneous requests. Only a 1-result/cycle architecture can meet the throughput constraint (>=32 Msps). Pipelined is the canonical 1-result/cycle family. The batch latency constraint (0.44us) is tight: at Fmax, N+2 cycles must fit in 0.44us. For N=12..18, latency is 14..20 cycles. At ~150-200 MHz, 20 cycles = 100-133ns, well under 440ns. Accuracy: max_abs_err <= 2^-12 = 0.000244. CORDIC error is dominated by residual angle after N iterations (~2^-N) and quantization. With data_width=14-18 and n_iter=12-18, we should be able to meet 2^-12 accuracy. The trade-off is between n_iter (more = better accuracy, more area/latency) and data_width (more = better quantization, more area). angle_guard and frac_guard add area for marginal accuracy gains; keep them low to minimize area. This family is the primary candidate for the Pareto front."
